<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
Output written to the console or to a log is commonly retained in shared log
storage, and is readable by operators, log aggregation services, and anyone else
with access to those logs. If a cryptographic key is written to such output, an
attacker who can read the logs can recover the key and use it to decrypt or forge
the data that it protects.
</p>
<p>
This query finds generated key material that is written to a log or to standard
output or standard error, possibly after hex encoding. Results from key-generation
APIs such as <code>KeyInit::generate_key</code> are already known to be keys, even
when returned without being passed to a constructor. Generic randomly filled
buffers, including results of helpers returning such buffers, must also flow to a
cryptographic key API. The standard
<code>rust/cleartext-logging</code> query relies on variable names to recognize
sensitive data, and does not recognize names such as <code>key_bytes</code>.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Do not write cryptographic keys to logs or to the console, even in encoded form. If
you need to identify a key in diagnostic output, log a non-secret identifier for
the key instead.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, a freshly generated AES-256-GCM key is hex-encoded and
printed:
</p>
<sample src="CleartextLoggingOfKeyMaterialBad.rs" />
<p>
In the following corrected example, only the key length is printed:
</p>
<sample src="CleartextLoggingOfKeyMaterialGood.rs" />
</example>

<section title="Known limitations">
<p>
For generic random buffers, both key-use and logging flows must be available from
the same source in the global data-flow graph. Data-flow state and operation order
can prevent some combinations from being correlated. The query does not infer this
relationship from variable names or use of the same helper.
</p>
<p>
Helpers are recognized only when the filled buffer flows locally to a return
value. Each call to such a helper is a separate source; logging inside that helper
and deeper chains of returning helpers are not covered by this call-site model.
</p>
</section>

<references>
<li>
MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html">CWE-532: Insertion of Sensitive Information into Log File</a>.
</li>
<li>
OWASP: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html">Logging Cheat Sheet</a>.
</li>
</references>

</qhelp>
